[1mBug fixed — [38;5;10mNode[0m struct padding in [38;5;10madd_node[0m[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C writes [38;5;10mNode new_node = {.id = ..., .parent_id = ..., .value = ..., .active = 1}[0m and then copies it into [38;5;10mnode_storage[0m. gcc compiles the partial designated initializer into a full 80-byte zero-fill (ten [38;5;10mmovq $0[0m stores, confirmed in [38;5;10m-S[0m output) before storing the named members, so the padding at offsets 58–63 (after [38;5;10mname[50][0m) and 76–79 (after [38;5;10mactive[0m) is deterministically zero in every stored node — and observable to any caller that copies a [38;5;10mNode[0m returned by [38;5;10mfind_node_by_id[0m. I verified this holds at [38;5;10m-O2[0m as well, so it isn't an artifact of the unoptimized CMake default.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust built the struct as a local literal and assigned it, leaving those padding bytes as stack garbage (visible in the debug build; the release build zeroed them only incidentally). The fix zeroes the destination slot with [38;5;10mptr::write_bytes[0m and then writes fields in place:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mlet slot: *mut Node = &raw mut storage()[count as usize];
std::ptr::write_bytes(slot as *mut u8, 0, std::mem::size_of::<Node>());
